what are the predictirs that sarta uses

The baseline statistical predictors used by SARTA (Stand-alone AIRS
Radiative Transfer Algorithm) are mathematical combinations of four
primary physical state variables: layer temperature (T), target gas
absorber amount (u), pressure (P), and path angle secant (θ).

Instead of passing raw variables directly, SARTA uses an expansion of
these inputs into a 10-to-15-term polynomial suite for each
atmospheric layer. This is done to linearize the highly non-linear
physics of molecular line absorption.

The exact predictors are divided into category groups based on the
type of gas being calculated:

1. Fixed Gas Predictors (For well-mixed gases like CO₂, O₂,
N₂O)Because well-mixed gases have relatively constant ratios in a
layer, their predictors isolate the effects of temperature variations
away from a reference standard profile (\(T_{ref}\)):

  \delta T: The absolute difference between the actual layer
  temperature and the reference profile temperature (\(T - T_{ref}\)).

  \((\delta T)^2\): The squared temperature variance, mapping
  non-linear thermal Doppler broadening.

  \(\sec(\theta)\): The secant of the local viewing zenith angle,
  scaling the geometric optical path length through the layer.

  \(\delta T \cdot \sec(\theta)\): An interaction term capturing
  path-dependent temperature changes.

2. Water Vapor Predictors (H₂O)

Water vapor is highly variable and dominates infrared absorption,
requiring a more intense set of predictors that blend temperature
offsets with water mass ratios

  (Q):\(Q / Q_{ref}\): The ratio of the actual water vapor column
  density to the reference standard profile.

  \(\delta T \cdot (Q / Q_{ref})\): Tracks how water vapor absorption
  behavior shifts with temperature.

  \((Q / Q_{ref}) \cdot \sec(\theta)\): Combines local water mass
  abundance with the slanted viewing path.

  \(\sqrt{Q/Q_{ref}}\): Evaluates the square root of the column ratio
  to linearize strong, saturated water lines.

3. Ozone Predictors (O₃)Similar to water vapor, ozone varies
dramatically by altitude layer (especially in the stratosphere) and
uses concentrated ratio expansions:

  \(V / V_{ref}\): The ratio of the actual layer ozone amount to the
  reference profile.

  \((V / V_{ref}) \cdot \sec(\theta)\): Scales ozone abundance along
  the slant path.

  \(\delta T \cdot (V / V_{ref})\): Evaluates the temperature
  dependence of specific ozone vibrational-rotational bands.4.

Cloud Scattering Predictors (When active)If SARTA is run in its cloudy
configurations, the regression framework adds macroscopic cloud
physical metrics to calculate scattering scaling factors:
  Cloud Top Pressure (\(P_{top}\))
  Cloud Optical Depth (τ)
  Effective Particle Radius (\(r_{e}\))

How They Synthesize an Output

SARTA evaluates these exact polynomial terms for every layer. It then
multiplies the results by a pre-calculated matrix of regression
coefficients (A) derived from line-by-line forward models (like
LBLRTM) to output the final channel optical depth

(\(\tau _{channel}\)):\(\tau _{channel}=\sum _{i=1}^{N}A_{i}\cdot \text{Predictor}_{i}\)

