Handling Variable Trace Gases (\(CO\) and \(CH_{4}\))

While fixed gases use a baseline 10-term polynomial mapping purely
temperature deviations, variable trace gases like Carbon Monoxide
(\(CO\)), Methane (\(CH_{4}\)), and Nitrous Oxide (\(N_{2}O\)) require
an advanced 7-to-11 term expansion framework.

Because trace gas concentrations fluctuate independently across
different atmospheric layers, their predictors must track the physical
abundance of the molecules alongside kinetic thermal changes. SARTA
sets up these specialized predictors using the actual layer gas column
density (\(q\)) against a standard reference profile abundance
(\(q_{ref}\)):

  Abundance Scaling: The ratio \(q/q_{ref}\) is used to scale baseline
  optical depth up or down depending on the local concentration.

  Path Concentration Interaction: The term \((q/q_{ref}) \cdot
  \sec(\theta)\) tracks how a slanted satellite viewing angle
  increases the effective number of trace gas molecules the sensor
  senses through that layer.

  Thermal-Abundance Pairing: The multi-variable term \(\Delta T \cdot
  (q/q_{ref})\) models how temperature variations alter the quantum
  vibrational-rotational absorption line intensities of that specific
  trace gas.

By processing these mixed terms, SARTA isolates individual gas
spectral signatures (such as the \(4.67\ \mu\text{m}\) carbon monoxide
absorption band or the \(7.6\ \mu\text{m}\) methane absorption band)
without re-running an entire line-by-line model setup.


2. From Layer Optical Depths to Top-of-Atmosphere (TOA) Radiance

Once SARTA computes the individual layer optical depths (\(\tau \))
for every gas across all 100 layers using the regression polynomials,
it executes a vertical integration to solve the Schwarzschild
Radiative Transfer Equation. This is what translates molecular
absorption into the exact energy structure detected by a satellite
sensor.

Step A: Layer-to-Space Transmittance (\(t\))

First, SARTA sums the individual optical depths of all active gases
(fixed, water vapor, ozone, and trace gases) inside a specific layer
(\(L\)) to find the total layer optical depth (\(\tau _{total}\)). It
converts this total into a layer-to-space transmittance profile
(\(t\)) using Beer's Law along the slanted path:\(t_{L}=\exp (-\tau
_{total}\cdot \sec (\theta ))\)Step B: Numerical Thermal
IntegrationWorking from the surface up to layer 1, SARTA accumulates
the total upwelling infrared energy. For a clear sky, the baseline
thermal stream consists of two primary parts:

  Surface Boundary Emission: The ground radiates energy upward based
  on its skin temperature (\(T_{skin}\)) and spectral emissivity
  (\(\varepsilon \)), multiplied by the total atmospheric
  transmittance from the surface to space:\(I_{surface}=\varepsilon
  \cdot B(\lambda ,T_{skin})\cdot t_{surface}\)
  (Where \(B\) represents the fundamental thermal Planck function)

  Atmospheric Layer Emission: Each individual atmospheric layer emits
  its own thermal radiation relative to its kinetic layer temperature
  (\(T_{L}\)). This emission is weighted by the layer's internal
  absorptivity (\(1 - t_{layer}\)) and then attenuated by the
  remaining upper atmosphere as it travels out to space.

