Are the water vapor continuum predictors the same as the ones for water that you showed above

The short answer is no, they are not exactly the same.

While they use the same fundamental atmospheric profile inputs
(temperature, water vapor mass, and viewing angle), SARTA
mathematically splits water vapor line absorption and water vapor
continuum absorption into two entirely separate, isolated calculation
loops with different polynomial terms.

The mathematical differences between the two sets highlight the
distinct physics each one isolates:

1. Water Vapor Line Predictors (The 11-Term Set From Earlier)

This set handles molecular quantum transitions (where a single water
molecule absorbs a very specific photon wavelength and vibrates or
rotates).

  The Math: Heavily features the square-root term (\(\sqrt{W_{r}}\))
  to model "line saturation." When a specific spectral line absorbs as
  much light as it physically can, adding more water vapor barely
  blocks any more light. The square root linearizes this flattening
  curve.

2. Water Vapor Continuum Predictors (A Targeted 4-to-5 Term Set)

The continuum handles smooth, broad background absorption across wide
swaths of the infrared spectrum. This is not caused by single isolated
molecules, but rather by molecular collisions (water molecules bumping
into each other, known as self-continuum, or water molecules bumping
into nitrogen/oxygen, known as foreign-continuum).

Because it is a collision-based phenomenon, the physics change
dramatically, and SARTA alters its predictor matrix to focus on:

  Pressure Products: It incorporates localized layer pressure terms
  (\(P\)) directly into the continuum predictors because higher
  pressure forces more frequent molecular collisions.

  Squared Abundance terms: Instead of scaling with a stabilizing
  square root (\(\sqrt{W_{r}}\)), the self-continuum scales with the
  square of the water vapor amount (\(W_{r}^{2}\)). If you double the
  number of water molecules in a layer, the number of collisions
  between them quadruples. SARTA's continuum loop specifically
  isolates these high-power terms to track this exponential drop in
  visibility.

Why split them up?

By isolating the line predictors from the continuum predictors, SARTA
can upgrade its background physics (for example, switching its
underlying continuum baseline to modern standards like the MT_CKD
model) without rewriting the entire 2,378-channel gas absorption
framework.

