yes combine WV with continuum

To combine water vapor line absorption and continuum absorption, SARTA
relies on a fundamental rule of radiative transfer: transmittances
multiply, while optical depths add.

SARTA performs separate polynomial calculations for the lines and the
continuum, and then combines them at the channel level for every
atmospheric layer.

Step 1: Separate Layer Computations

For any given atmospheric layer (L) and instrument channel, SARTA
concurrently solves two distinct regression equations:

  Line Optical Depth (\(\tau _{\text{line}}\)): Calculated using the
  11-term polynomial set (incorporating the \(\sqrt{W_{r}}\)
  saturation terms).

  Continuum Optical Depth (\(\tau _{\text{cont}}\)): Calculated using
  the collision-based polynomial set (incorporating the \(W_{r}^{2}\)
  and layer pressure terms).

Step 2: Combining the Optical Depths (The Additive Step)

Because optical depth is a linear measure of total molecular
cross-section, SARTA simply adds the two values together to get the
Total Water Vapor Optical Depth (\(\tau _{\text{H2O}}\)) for that
layer:

  \(\tau _{\text{H2O}}(L)=\tau _{\text{line}}(L)+\tau
  _{\text{cont}}(L)\)

If other gases absorb light in that same channel (such as a fixed gas
like CO₂ or ozone), their calculated optical depths are added to the
sum as well:

  \(\tau _{\text{Total}}(L)=\tau _{\text{H2O}}(L)+\tau
  _{\text{Fixed}}(L)+\tau _{\text{O3}}(L)\)

Step 3: Transformation to Transmittance (The Multiplicative Step)

Once the total optical depth for the layer is established, SARTA
applies Beer’s Law along the slanted satellite path (\(\sec\theta\))
to compute the Layer Transmittance (\(t_{\text{layer}}\)).

Mathematically, passing the added optical depths through an
exponential function automatically turns the relationship into a
multiplication of transmittances

  \(t_{\text{layer}}=\exp (-\tau _{\text{Total}}\cdot \sec \theta
  )=\exp (-\tau _{\text{line}}\cdot \sec \theta )\times \exp (-\tau
  _{\text{cont}}\cdot \sec \theta )\times \exp (-\tau
  _{\text{other}}\cdot \sec \theta )\)

  \(t_{\text{layer}}=t_{\text{line}}\times t_{\text{cont}}\times
  t_{\text{other}}\)

Why this combination matters for Satellite Imagery

This combination directly dictates what a satellite sensor sees:

  In the Atmospheric Infrared Windows (e.g., 8–12 μm): Water vapor
  line absorption (\(\tau _{\text{line}}\)) is very weak, but the
  continuum (\(\tau _{\text{cont}}\)) is highly active. In humid
  tropical regions, the \(W_{r}^{2}\) continuum term dominates,
  lowering the total transmittance (\(t_{\text{layer}}\)) and hiding
  the true temperature of the Earth's surface.

  In the Water Vapor Absorption Bands (e.g., 6.3 μm): The line
  absorption (\(\tau _{\text{line}}\)) is incredibly strong and
  saturates high up in the troposphere. The continuum math becomes
  secondary because \(t_{\text{line}}\) drops to near zero, completely
  blocking any thermal radiation from reaching space from the lower
  layers.

Once SARTA calculates this final combined layer transmittance
(\(t_{\text{layer}}\)) for all 100 levels, it passes the vertical grid
directly to the numerical integration engine to build the
top-of-atmosphere radiances.

