When an atmospheric profile hits extreme winter (intense cold and dry
air) or extreme summer (intense heat and high humidity), SARTA’s
restricted polynomial framework (relying only on linear terms,
squares, and square roots) undergoes two distinct tests: thermodynamic
boundaries and coefficient training limits.

1. Behavior in Extreme Winter Limits (The Cold/Dry Test)

In environments like polar regions or the high stratosphere,
temperatures drop far below the reference profile, making \(\Delta T\)
a large negative number. Simultaneously, water vapor mass drops to
near zero (\(W_r \approx 0\)).

  Preventing Negative Optical Depths: Because SARTA lacks erratic
  cubic or quartic terms, the quadratic term \((\Delta T)^2\) always
  yields a stable, predictable positive value. It cannot flip the sign
  of the overall calculation.

  The Line Saturation Collapse: As moisture vanishes, the square root
  term \(\sqrt{W_{r}}\) drops smoothly toward zero without causing a
  "divide-by-zero" computational error. The line absorption loop
  effectively shuts down cleanly, allowing the satellite channels to
  see straight through to the frozen surface below.

2. Behavior in Extreme Summer Limits (The Hot/Humid Test)

In tropical marine environments, temperatures soar and humidity
skyrockets, pushing water vapor levels far above the reference
standard (\(W_r \gg 1\)).

  The Continuum Explosion: This is where the squared term
  (\(W_{r}^{2}\)) comes into play. Because collision-based absorption
  scales with the square of the mass, a high summer humidity value
  causes the continuum optical depth (\(\tau _{\text{cont}}\)) to grow
  exponentially. SARTA accurately captures the atmospheric "greenhouse
  blanketing" that completely masks the surface boundary in thermal
  window channels.

  Controlled Extrapolation: Because the polynomial order is kept low
  (quadratic), if a historic heatwave pushes temperatures slightly
  outside the model's design specifications, the curve continues along
  a smooth parabola rather than oscillating wildly.

How the Coefficients are Trained for Boundaries

To ensure that these squares and square roots behave properly at the
edges of reality, the static regression coefficients (\(A_{i}\)) must
be trained using a diverse, highly specific database of global
conditions.

The UMBC Atmospheric Spectroscopy Laboratory trains SARTA using a
48-Profile or 52-Profile Training Set:

  Not a Simple Average: The training database does not just use
  average global weather. Instead, it deliberately samples the
  absolute statistical extremes of the planet.

  The Grid Matrix: It includes profiles from the Sahara Desert at
  noon, the Antarctic interior in winter, tropical super-cell
  environments, and high-altitude mountain regions.

  The Tuning Pass: By running slow, hyper-accurate line-by-line models
  (LBLRTM) on these 52 extreme profiles across thousands of viewing
  angles, they generate a diverse dataset. The SARTA regression
  coefficients are then solved using a least-squares fit against this
  database.

Because the training set covers the absolute physical boundaries of
Earth's atmosphere, and because the predictors are restricted to
stable squares and square roots, SARTA can safely process real-time
weather data from any spot on the globe without crashing or generating
unrealistic brightness temperatures.

